intro = [
 "In western New York in the 1820s, a farm boy named Joseph Smith later said he had prayed in a wood and seen God and Jesus Christ, and that three years afterwards an angel showed him gold plates buried in a hill. From those claims came the Book of Mormon (March 1830), a church organised in April 1830, and a movement that by 1844 had built a city on the Mississippi and lost its founder to a mob at Carthage jail.",
 "The heart of this chapter is a dating problem. No document from 1820 mentions the vision. Smith told it in at least four accounts of his own: a manuscript history of about summer 1832, the earliest and the only one partly in his hand; a journal entry of 9 November 1835, told to a visitor; the formal history begun in 1838 and drafted in 1839, later canonised; and the Wentworth letter printed on 1 March 1842. They differ in his age (fourteen or 'in his sixteenth year'), in whether one personage or two appeared, in angels, and in the message. They are set here in the order in which they were written. Latter-day Saints read them as one experience told for different audiences; critics read them as a story that grew; this chapter states both as positions.",
 "Around Smith stand the people who wrote down what they saw or heard: his mother Lucy, dictating twenty years later; his scribe Oliver Cowdery, whose 1834–35 letters were the first published history and date a revival to 1823; Martin Harris and the ten other men who signed the testimonies printed in the book; and Emma Smith, interviewed by her son in the last months of her life. Against them stand the sceptics and officials: a Bainbridge justice's bill of costs from 1826, the earliest dated record of Smith, which concerns treasure-seeking with a stone; Professor Charles Anthon's letters denying he ever vouched for the characters; Emma's father Isaac Hale's sworn statement; Alexander Campbell's 1831 review; and E. D. Howe's 1834 book of neighbours' affidavits.",
 "Very little of this is diary. The honest record is mostly later recollections, letters, affidavits, signed testimonies, court bills and newspapers, each tagged here by kind and by the years between the event and the writing. Willard Richards's terse journal from Carthage jail is the rare exception: a record made by the hour. The killings of Joseph and Hyrum Smith on 27 June 1844 are told through Richards, Governor Thomas Ford, who had pledged their safety, and their mother.",
 "No verdict is offered on whether anything supernatural happened. Believers' own terms appear in their words; elsewhere the narration uses plain names. What can be laid on one timeline is who said what, when they wrote it down, and what followed.",
]

